## 🚀 Quick Start

> **Explore, choose a project, and start playing or learning.**

### 1️⃣ Open the collection
Use the **Explore Games** button at the top of this README to visit the provided resource.

### 2️⃣ Choose a game or project
Browse the available entries and check each project's description, prerequisites, and usage instructions.

### 3️⃣ Play or examine the source
If a playable demo is provided, open it in a compatible browser. If source files are provided, follow that project's own README to run it locally.

<br/>

<img src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/andreasbm/readme/master/assets/lines/rainbow.png" width="100%" alt="section divider"/>

## ⚙️ How HTML5 Games Work

<div align="center">

| Technology | Typical Role | Examples |
|:---|:---|:---|
| 🧱 **HTML5** | Page structure and browser APIs | `canvas`, elements, media |
| 🎨 **CSS3** | Layout, styling, and visual effects | Responsive UI, transitions |
| ⚡ **JavaScript** | Game behavior and interaction | Input handling, collisions, scoring |
| 🖼️ **Canvas / WebGL** | Rendering graphics | 2D scenes and 3D experiences |
| 🔊 **Web Audio** | Sound and feedback | Music, effects, audio cues |

</div>

Different games may use different frameworks, build tools, and runtime requirements. Always consult the documentation included with the specific project you want to run.

